{/*
  The black/blue page-transition curtain from the original one-page-layout
  AJAX system. This component is just two <div>s (below) — all the actual
  animation logic lives in two other files that work together with it:
    - CSS classes below control what "covered" vs "revealed" look like.
    - src/scripts/transitions.ts decides WHEN to switch between them,
      timed against Astro's page-navigation events.

  How the two overlay divs create the wipe effect:
    overlay-1 is solid black, overlay-2 is solid blue, both are full-screen
    and stacked on top of each other with a tiny timing offset (the
    `transition-delay` values below). Sliding both off-screen with that
    stagger is what makes it read as one solid color "handing off" to the
    other, rather than two flat color swaps.

  Every page starts "covered" (rendered server-side, so there's no flash of
  uncovered content before JS runs), then wipes away shortly after load.
*/}
<div class="curtain curtain-cover curtain-instant">
  <div class="overlay overlay-1"></div>
  <div class="overlay overlay-2"></div>
</div>

<style>
  /* "covered" state: both overlays sit at their natural position (0%),
     fully hiding the page underneath. */
  .curtain-cover .overlay-1 {
    transform: translateY(0%);
    transition-delay: 0s;
  }
  .curtain-cover .overlay-2 {
    transform: translateY(0%);
    transition-delay: 0.15s;
  }
  /* "revealed" state: both overlays slide fully off-screen (100% of their
     own height, downward). The staggered transition-delay above is what
     makes overlay-1 (black) start moving a beat before overlay-2 (blue)
     catches up and finishes the wipe. */
  .curtain-reveal .overlay-1 {
    transform: translateY(100%);
    transition-delay: 0.15s;
  }
  .curtain-reveal .overlay-2 {
    transform: translateY(100%);
    transition-delay: 0s;
  }
  /* Used only on the very first paint of a page: skips the animation
     entirely (transition-duration: 0) so the curtain just *appears*
     already covering, instead of visibly sliding in from nowhere. */
  .curtain-instant .overlay {
    transition-duration: 0s !important;
    transition-delay: 0s !important;
  }
</style>

<style is:global>
  /* This curtain is the only visual transition we want — Astro's client
     router (astro:transitions) otherwise applies its own default crossfade
     via the View Transition API, which fights our manual cover/reveal
     timing and can leave a stale snapshot on screen. Turning the browser's
     own root transition into a no-op animation leaves 100% of the visual
     handoff to the curtain. */
  ::view-transition-old(root),
  ::view-transition-new(root) {
    animation: none;
    mix-blend-mode: normal;
  }
</style>
